00:01:46.117 --> 00:01:47.880
"Why does cutting my hand with scissors

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.200
hurt less than a paper cut?"

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:50.610
Oh, that's such a good question.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:51.443
First of all,

00:01:51.443 --> 00:01:53.880
I don't think that's true.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.170
Just saying as a doctor.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:58.230
I think just the paper
cut is less expected.

00:01:58.230 --> 00:01:59.790
Like if scissors are cutting you,

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:01.440
you expect large amounts of pain

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:02.610
so you kind of prepare for it.

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:05.100
But just like getting a hit unexpectedly,

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.110
like getting sucker punch or
something hurts much worse,

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.130
a paper cut,

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:09.000
you're like,
"Oh, this is a paper.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:09.833
No big deal."

00:02:09.833 --> 00:02:10.666
Ugh, it cut me!

00:02:10.666 --> 00:02:13.410
That ugh-it-cut-me
moment could be an issue.

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:17.250
Our pain perception is not
solely decided by the injury

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:18.780
but also our perception of the injury.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.990
We basically have like a volume
remote of our pain signals.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:24.660
But if we can learn to dial
down the volume on the pain,

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:25.860
we can get a better control of it

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.037
without taking medications.

